*Running*

In order to run this compiler, you first need to run "make" or "make compiler" in the terminal.
Then run the compiler file like "./compiler < 'file.cpm'"
This will print alot of information in the terminal about the program, aswell as creating the bytecode.txt file that contains the Bytecode information the interpreter needs to run the program.

Now you need the interpreter that you need to run "make interpreter" in the terminal.
Then you need to run the interpreter file like this "./interpreter 'bytecode.txt'" (notice the missing '<'). This runs the program.

*Debug*

If something goes wrong in the interpreter you can enable debug in [a relative link](interpreter.cpp), then recompiling the interpreter. This prints the data stack, local variables and currently running bytecode instruction. 

This should help you diagnose faults in the bytecode or the interpreter just as much as it helped me make it.

*Testing*

Made a bunch of AI-made unit tests in the "filip_test_files/AI_tests" directory. 
The tests are for invalid code, and the expected errors are in the comments. 

In order to run the tests, run "make test" in the terminal. This will run both the AI tests and the
standard tests.

The results of the tests will be in the "test_results/summary.txt" directory. 

*Bytecode explanation for c+-*

The bytecode most contain no whitespaces or blank lines. Newline is to indicate a new line to the interpreters parser.

All c+- bytecode files start with "CPMBC" which stands for c+- bytecode. This is the header.

(.) indicates a new class
(#) indicates a new method
(-) indicates a new block

After the (./#/-) you declare the name of the class/method/block.

All methods and classes NEED to start with the variable map even if there are no field or local variables. This is done by starting the variable map with "VMSTART" and ending it with "VMEND". Between these is the variables the function or class uses written as "variable-name:index". Index must be numerical and unique, non unique indexes is undefined behavior. 

For classes after the variable map comes the definisions for the methods.

In the method after the variable map you start the methods functionality with "START" and end it with "END".

Between "START" and "END", you declare the blocks. A block starts with the true edge then false edge. These are the edges you can jump to in the block, they are the names of the block you want to jump to. "JUMP_IF_FALSE" bytecode instruction is supposed to jump to the false block and "JUMP" is supposed to jump to the true block. No edge is declared as "null".

Then after the true and false edge have been declared now comes the bytecode instructions.

they are defined as (Bytecode_instruction:arg1:arg2:arg1_string:arg2_string).
The string args are only functionally used in class bytecode instructions, jumps and debug. 
"arg1" mainly refers to the var map or the (int bool or float representation in an int). 
"arg2" represent a secondary argument some bytecode instructions have like "CALL_METHOD".
Some instructions like "ADD", "PRINT" or "ARRAY_LOAD" do not require any arguments in the bytecode part since they take their arguments from the data stack.

all bytecode instructions:

CONST_INT: push int to stack, ex. CONST_INT:1:0:1:
CONST_FLOAT: push float to stack, ex. CONST_FLOAT:1075419546:0:2.400000:
CONST_BOOL: push boolean to stack, ex. CONST_BOOL:1:0:true:

LOAD_LOCAL: push local variable to stack, ex. LOAD_LOCAL:3:0:variable:
STORE_LOCAL: pop stack into local variable, ex. STORE_LOCAL:3:0:variable:

ADD: pop two, push sum, ex. ADD::::
SUB: pop two, push difference, ex. SUB::::
MUL: pop two, push product, ex. MUL::::
DIV: pop two, push quotient, ex. DIV::::
POW: pop two, push exponentiation, ex. POW::::

AND: pop two, push logical and, ex. AND::::
OR: pop two, push logical or, ex. OR::::
NOT: pop one, push logical not, ex. NOT::::

CMP_EQ: pop two, push equality result, ex. CMP_EQ::::
CMP_NEQ: pop two, push inequality result, ex. CMP_NEQ::::
CMP_LT: pop two, push less-than result, ex. CMP_LT::::
CMP_LTE: pop two, push less-or-equal result, ex. CMP_LTE::::
CMP_GT: pop two, push greater-than result, ex. CMP_GT::::
CMP_GTE: pop two, push greater-or-equal result, ex. CMP_GTE::::

JUMP: jump to target block, ex. JUMP:::BLOCK_3:
JUMP_IF_FALSE: pop one, jump to target block if false, ex. JUMP_IF_FALSE:::BLOCK_3:

CALL_METHOD: pop args and object, push old frame to frame stack, make frame new frame, ex. CALL_METHOD::5:METHOD:5
RETURN: restore frame and ends program if no frame left on framestack, ex. RETURN::::
RETURN_VOID: restore frame, ex. RETURN_VOID::::

NEW_OBJECT: push new object, ex. NEW_OBJECT:::CLASS:
LOAD_FIELD: pop object, push field value, ex. LOAD_FIELD:1:0:fieldname:
STORE_FIELD: pop value and object, store field, ex. STORE_FIELD:1:0:fieldname:

NEW_ARRAY: pop size, push new array, ex. NEW_ARRAY::::
ARRAY_LOAD: pop index and array, push element, ex. ARRAY_LOAD::::
ARRAY_STORE: pop value, index, array, store element, ex. ARRAY_STORE::::
ARRAY_LENGTH: pop array, push length, ex. ARRAY_LENGTH::::

PRINT: pop value, print it, ex. PRINT::::
READ: read input, push value, ex. READ::::

HALT: terminate execution, ex. HALT::::